source><year>1996</year><volume>435</volume><fpage>1219</fpage><lpage>1227</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Little</surname><given-names>RJA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Robust estimation of the mean and covariance matrix from data with missing values</article-title><source>Applied Statistical</source><year>1988</year><volume>37</volume><fpage>23</fpage><lpage>39</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pinheiro</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Efficient algorithms for robust estimation in linear mixed-effects models using the multivariate t distribution</article-title><source>J. Comput. Graph. Stat.</source><year>2001</year><volume>10</volume><fpage>249</fpage><lpage>276</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Plummer</surname><given-names>MN</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><source>Output analysis and diagnostics for MCMC, v. 0.9&#x02013;5.</source><year>2004</year><comment>Available at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www-fis.iarc.fr/coda/">http://www-fis.iarc.fr/coda/</ext-link>(last accessed date March 2006)</comment></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Raftery</surname><given-names>AE</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Bayesian model averaging for linear regression models</article-title><source>J. Am. Stat. Assoc.</source><year>1997</year><volume>92</volume><fpage>179</fpage><lpage>191</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Reba&#x000ef;</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Comparison of methods for regression interval mapping in QTL analysis with non-normal traits</article-title><source>Genet. Res.</source><year>1997</year><volume>69</volume><fpage>69</fpage><lpage>74</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ripley</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><source>Stochastic Simulation</source><year>1987</year><publisher-loc>New York</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Wiley</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rogers</surname><given-names>WH</given-names></name><name><surname>Tukey</surname><given-names>JW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Understanding some long-tailed distributions</article-title><source>Stat. Neerl.</source><year>1972</year><volume>26</volume><fpage>211</fpage><lpage>226</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rohr</surname><given-names>PV</given-names></name><name><surname>Hoeschele</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Bayesian QTL mapping using skewed Student-t distributions</article-title><source>Genet. Sel. Evol.</source><year>2002</year><volume>34</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>21</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11929622</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rosa</surname><given-names>GJM</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Robust linear mixed models with Normal/Independent distributions and Bayesian MCMC implementation</article-title><source>Biom. J.</source><year>2003</year><volume>5</volume><fpage>573</fpage><lpage>590</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B27"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rosa</surname><given-names>GJM</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Bayesian longitudinal data analysis with mixed models and thick-tailed distributions using MCMC</article-title><source>J. Appl. Stat.</source><year>2004</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>855</fpage><lpage>873</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sillanp&#x000e4;&#x000e4;</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Corander</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Model choice in gene mapping: what and why</article-title><source>Trends Genet</source><year>2002</year><volume>18</volume><fpage>301</fpage><lpage>307</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12044359</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B29"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sokal</surname><given-names>RR</given-names></name><name><surname>Rohlf</surname><given-names>FJ</given-names></name></person-group><source>Biometry: The Principles and Practice of Statistics in Biological Research.</source><year>1995</year><publisher-loc>New York</publisher-loc><publisher-name>W.H. Freeman and Company</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B30"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Symons</surname><given-names>RC</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Multiple genetic loci modify susceptibility to plasmacytoma-related morbidity in E&#x003bc;-v-abl transgenic mice</article-title><source>Proc. Natl Acad. Sci. USA</source><year>2002</year><volume>99</volume><fpage>11299</fpage><lpage>11304</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12149518</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B31"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Bayesian shrinkage estimation of quantitative trait loci parameters</article-title><source>Genetics</source><year>2005</year><volume>170</volume><fpage>465</fpage><lpage>480</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15781696</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B32"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yang</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Box&#x02013;Cox transformation for QTL mapping</article-title><source>Genetica</source><year>2006</year><volume>128</volume><fpage>133</fpage><lpage>143</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17028946</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B33"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yi</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Bayesian model selection for genome-wide epistatic quantitative trait loci analysis</article-title><source>Genetics</source><year>2005</year><volume>170</volume><fpage>1333</fpage><lpage>1344</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15911579</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B34"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yi</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Bayesian mapping of genomewide interacting quantitative trait loci for ordinal traits</article-title><source>Genetics</source><year>2007</year><volume>176</volume><fpage>1855</fpage><lpage>1864</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17507680</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B35"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>YM</given-names></name><name><surname>Xu</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Advanced statistical methods for detecting multiple quantitative trait loci</article-title><source>Recent Res. Devel. Genet. Breed.</source><year>2005</year><volume>2</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>23</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B36"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zou</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Rank-based statistical methodologies for quantitative trait locus mapping</article-title><source>Genetics</source><year>2003</year><volume>165</volume><fpage>1599</fpage><lpage>1605</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14668406</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><app-group><app id="APP1"><title>Appendix A</title><p>Specification of prior for degree of freedom <italic>df</italic> in normal/independent distributions</p><p>In the <italic>t</italic>-distribution, we adopt a flat prior for <italic>df</italic> as <italic>df</italic><sup>&#x02212;1</sup>, yielding <italic>p</italic>(<italic>df</italic>)&#x0221d;<italic>df</italic><sup>&#x02212;2</sup> (Liu, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B17">1996</xref>); A Gamma(<italic>a</italic>, <italic>b</italic>) distribution with small positive values of a and b (<italic>b</italic>&#x0226a;<italic>a</italic>)can be adopted as a prior for <italic>df</italic> in the slash distribution; and the prior for <italic>df</italic> of contaminated normal distribution involves two parameters, i.e. <italic>df</italic>=(<italic>v</italic> &#x003c4;). Herein, a Uniform (0, 1) distribution is used as a prior for &#x003c4; and an independent Beta (<italic>a</italic>, <italic>b</italic>) is adopted as prior a for <italic>v</italic>.</p></app><app id="APP2"><title>Appendix B</title><p>Forms of posteriors for <italic>w</italic> and degree of freedom <italic>df</italic> in normal/independent distributions</p><p>For a <italic>t</italic>-distribution, the fully conditional posterior density for each element of <italic>w</italic> is a Gamma distribution with parameters <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn558i14.jpg"/></inline-formula> and <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn558i15.jpg"/></inline-formula>, corresponding conditional posterior density of <italic>df</italic> is<disp-formula><graphic xlink:href="btn558um6.jpg" position="float"/></disp-formula>which does not have an explicit form but a Metropolis/Hastings or rejection sampling step (Ripley, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B23">1987</xref>) can be embedded in the MCMC scheme to obtain draws for <italic>df</italic>.</p><p>For slash distribution,<inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn558i16.jpg"/></inline-formula></p><p>For contaminated normal distribution, the fully conditional posterior density for <italic>w</italic><sub><italic>i</italic></sub> is also non-closed form: <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn558i17.jpg"/></inline-formula> with <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn558i18.jpg"/></inline-formula>.</p></app></app-group></back></article>